      mechanics: "Section 5: Serial Communication Interfaces & Display Fabrics\nTransferring byte sequences between two isolated microcontrollers over a minimal 2-wire bus layout (TX and RX lines) without sharing a common master clock timing signal line.\nThe Frame Constraints: Because there is no shared clock line, both devices must be hard-configured to execute at an identical Baud Rate (bit transmission speed, e.g., 9600 or 115200 bits per second).\nThe Bitstream Sequence: The transmission line rests permanently at an idle logic high voltage (V_DD). Communication initiates when the transmitter drops the line low for exactly one bit period, creating the Start Bit to wake up the receiver's internal counter. This is followed immediately by 5 to 8 Data Bits, an optional Parity Bit for basic 1-bit error checking, and terminates with 1 or 2 logic high Stop Bits to return the line to the idle state.",
      analogy: "Imagine two people communicating across mountains using flashlights at night. They don't have a shared metronome ticking the time. Instead, they agree on a strict speed rule: 'Every single flash or pause lasts exactly 1 second (The Baud Rate).' The line rests dark. To start a message, the transmitter flashes the light ON for exactly 1 second (The Start Bit) to tell the receiver to start counting seconds. The receiver ticks their internal stopwatch, reading the light status every second for the next 8 intervals to decipher the letter code, followed by a double-second pause to signal completion."
