      mechanics: "Section 3: High-Performance Memory Subsystems & Cache Engineering\nIn multi-core computing systems, every individual CPU core has its own private L1 cache array, but they all share access to a single master main memory bus. If Core 0 modifies a variable inside its private L1 cache, the copy of that variable inside Core 1's L1 cache instantly becomes stale and corrupted. To prevent this, the hardware implements an active Snooping Cache Coherence Protocol governed by an internal Finite State Machine.\nThe MESI State Framework:\n• Modified (M): The data is valid, but it is present only in the local cache and contains modified data that has not yet been written back to main memory.\n• Exclusive (E): The data matches main memory perfectly and is present only in the local cache.\n• Shared (S): The data matches main memory perfectly, but is also present concurrently inside the private caches of other cores.\n• Invalid (I): The data block contains stale or invalid data and cannot be read.\nThe Bus Snooping Event: If Core 0 wants to write to a block marked as Shared, it must broadcast an 'Invalidation Signal' across the shared memory bus line. Core 1's cache controller continuously listens to the bus; when it catches that invalidation signal, it automatically flips its local block status flag from Shared to Invalid, guaranteeing data consistency across the multi-core chip.",
