      analogy: "Imagine two people communicating across a raucous, vibrating rock concert stadium floor using two parallel long ropes held in their hands. If they agree to measure the height of a single rope against the floor, the massive ambient floor vibrations will warp the measurements completely, ruining the message. Instead, they use differential signaling: to transmit a message bit, they yank the ropes away from each other—pulling Rope A up toward the ceiling and slamming Rope B flat to the ground (Dominant State, 0). The receiver doesn't check the floor distance; they simply measure the physical gap between the two ropes. If a giant earthquake hits the building, both ropes lift together, but the gap distance between them stays perfectly constant, allowing the message code to pass through the chaos perfectly."
